---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { facts, timeline, skills } from '../data/site';
---
<Base title="About" description="Shyamnath Sankar is a co-founder of Genrecai and a Computer Science & Business Systems student in Chennai who builds AI products from idea to daily use.">
  <section class="page-hero" aria-labelledby="about-title" data-signal="0.1">
    <p class="page-hero__kicker rise">About</p>
    <h1 class="page-hero__title page-hero__title--long rise" id="about-title" style="--d: 0.06s">From the first messy idea to the day someone depends on it.</h1>
  </section>

  <section class="bio" aria-labelledby="bio-h" data-signal="0.35">
    <h2 class="sr-only" id="bio-h">Biography</h2>
    <div class="bio__card">
      <p class="bio__card-head"><span class="bio__dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>Profile</p>
      <dl>
        <div><dt>Based</dt><dd>Chennai, India</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Co-founder, PM & lead AI dev, Genrecai</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Study</dt><dd>B.Tech CSBS, V.S.B. Engineering, 2027</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Builds</dt><dd>RAG, agents, MCP, full stack</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Contact</dt><dd><a class="link" href="mailto:shyamnathsankar.s@gmail.com">Email</a></dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
    <div class="bio__text">
      <p class="bio__lead" data-split="lines">I’m Shyamnath Sankar. I study Computer Science & Business Systems at V.S.B. Engineering College and co-founded Genrecai, where I’m the product manager and lead AI developer.</p>
      <p data-reveal="up">My work sits on both sides of the product. On one side, the engineering: retrieval pipelines that answer from the source, agent graphs that know when to stop, MCP servers that let coding agents use a real browser. On the other, the decisions: what to build first, what to leave out, and how a screen should feel to someone who has never heard the word embedding.</p>
      <p data-reveal="up">LuminaIQ was picked among the top 150 global startups at StartupGrind 2026. AnyLLM earned its first revenue within a month on Google Play. EduEcho runs feedback for my whole college. I also ship open-source tools like HailBrowser and SelectStar.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="why" aria-labelledby="why-h" data-chapter-block="paper" data-signal="1">
    <ol class="why__log" aria-label="How I work">
      <li><span class="why__no">01</span><span>Find the one question the product must answer.</span></li>
      <li><span class="why__no">02</span><span>Build the retrieval, the agents and the screens around it.</span></li>
      <li><span class="why__no">03</span><span>Show every step, so people can check the answer.</span></li>
      <li><span class="why__no">04</span><span>Ship small, then keep going until someone depends on it.</span></li>
    </ol>
    <div class="why__text">
      <h2 class="why__h" id="why-h" data-split="lines">From noise to signal</h2>
      <p data-reveal="up">Most of what I build starts as noise: a stack of PDFs, a spreadsheet nobody trusts, a database only one person can query, a web that agents can’t read. The work is turning that into a clear answer, fast enough that people use it every day.</p>
      <p data-reveal="up">The lines behind this site show the same idea. They wander in the open sections and straighten into parallel signal wherever the work is.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="now" aria-labelledby="now-h" data-signal="0.5">
    <h2 class="now__h" id="now-h" data-split="lines">Now and next</h2>
    <ol class="now__list">
      {timeline.map((t) => (
        <li class="now__row" data-reveal="up">
          <span class="now__when">{t.when}</span>
          <span class="now__what">{t.what}</span>
          <span class="now__where">{t.where}</span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="skills" aria-labelledby="skills-h">
    <h2 class="skills__h" id="skills-h" data-split="lines">What I work with</h2>
    <div class="skills__grid">
      {skills.map((s) => (
        <div class="skills__col" data-reveal="up">
          <h3>{s.group}</h3>
          <ul>{s.items.map((i) => <li>{i}</li>)}</ul>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="facts facts--about" aria-labelledby="facts-h">
    <h2 class="facts__h" id="facts-h" data-split="lines">The record so far</h2>
    <dl class="facts__list">
      {facts.map((f) => (
        <div class="fact" data-reveal="up"><dt>{f.label}</dt><dd>{f.value}</dd></div>
      ))}
    </dl>
  </section>
</Base>
